{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792309037,"nonce":"/B0MohQst/+U8UsfmrlT0gsJaxo=","message":"Findings from the ChimpMonk dumps (16-panel stage, 7512 used blocks = 235 MB, 40.7-43 MB live): 84% of blocks are under a quarter full (mean 44 live lines of 256), only 2 are empty. Page-level handback is ruled out: with the census-pages lines (f0c7225f, branch gc-page-census, not on main), 92% of 16 KB pages (13768 of 15024) hold a live line, so dead lines are sprinkled between survivors and handback would return about 8%; the bare workspace is at 98%. blocks-reusable 0 is not a signal: that set is blocks already handed back to the OS; hole refill is line recycling (recycle_spans, on by default). Moving objects is not available: tracing is conservative, so a pointer cannot be rewritten safely. Remaining lever is the collection trigger: trigger = retained-block bytes x growth (default 4) clamped to 64..512 MB, so it was 512 MB in the dump and only about one collection ran during a 240 MB burst. Two things to try: ASH_GC_GROWTH=1/2 on their probe (no build needed), and scaling the trigger from marked bytes rather than retained-block bytes, which breaks the loop where fragmentation raises the next threshold. Benchmarks (binary_trees was the tuning case for growth 4) need the NUC or CI before changing a default.","files":null}]}